<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=sputnik-1">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Transport and navigation</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sputnik 1</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1957">1957</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sputnik 1 was the first artificial Earth satellite, launched by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> from Baikonur on 4 October 1957. A polished sphere with four antennas, it transmitted radio signals for three weeks and burned up in the atmosphere in January 1958. Its launch shocked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and began the space race.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sputnik-1.jpg" alt="Sputnik 1" width="320" height="431" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: U.S. Air Force photo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sputnik_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>On 17 December 1954 the Soviet rocket scientist Sergei Korolev proposed a plan for an artificial satellite to the Minister of the Defense Industry, Dimitri Ustinov. In July 1955 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> announced that it would launch a satellite during the International Geophysical Year, and four days later the Soviet physicist Leonid Sedov said the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> would do the same. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/politburo">Politburo</a> approved the proposal on 8 August 1955. The launch vehicle was the R-7, designed as an intercontinental ballistic missile. A much heavier satellite, Object D, was planned first, but the lighter Object PS, which became Sputnik 1, was launched ahead of it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Sputnik 1 was a polished sphere 58 centimetres across, assembled from two hemispheres sealed with O-rings and joined by 36 bolts, with a mass of 83.6 kilograms. Its chief constructor at OKB-1 was Mikhail Khomyakov. Four whip antennas gave it an almost spherical radiation pattern. It could not be controlled, only observed. A spherical body let scientists work out the density of the upper atmosphere from the satellite's lifetime in orbit, and its radio signals were meant to give data on how radio waves travel through the atmosphere.</p><p>It was launched from Tyuratam in the Kazakh SSR on 4 October 1957 at 19:28:34 UTC, which was 5 October at the launch site. Several engines did not fire on time, which nearly aborted the mission, and the satellite entered an orbit with an apogee about 500 kilometres lower than intended. It circled the Earth every 96.20 minutes at a peak speed of about 8 kilometres a second. It transmitted on 20.005 and 40.002 MHz for 22 days until the batteries ran out on 26 October. On 4 January 1958 it burned up on re-entering the atmosphere, after 1,440 orbits.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Radio operators around the world picked up the beeping signal, and the first recording in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> was made by RCA engineers on Long Island. Groups of observers at 150 stations in Operation Moonwatch looked for the satellite in the sky. What most people actually saw was the much more visible core stage of the rocket. The Soviets did not at once use the launch for propaganda, but later stressed the achievement as a sign of their superiority.</p><p>President Dwight Eisenhower was not surprised, having been forewarned of the R-7's capabilities, but he underestimated the reaction of the American public, who were shocked by the launch and by the televised failure of the Vanguard rocket. The anxiety was sometimes called the Sputnik craze, and the launch began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/space-race">Space Race</a>. Two physicists at Johns Hopkins University, William Guier and George Weiffenbach, used the Doppler effect in Sputnik's signal to fix its position, which led to the TRANSIT navigation system, a forerunner of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/global-positioning-system">Global Positioning System</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sputnik 1” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its achievements in science and technology were real: it launched the first artificial satellite, <b>Sputnik 1</b>, in October 1957 and the first man in space, Yuri Gagarin, on 12 April 1961.</blockquote><a href="/empire/soviet-union">Soviet Union · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Soviet Union placed <b>Sputnik 1</b>, an 84-kilogram metal sphere, into orbit, the first artificial satellite.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-4">4 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Soviet nuclear test site near Semipalatinsk carried out over 450 tests from 1949 to 1989, and Baikonur launched <b>Sputnik</b> and Yuri Gagarin.</blockquote><a href="/state/kazakh-ssr">Kazakh SSR</a></li></ul></section>
